\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  a) An overview of the reproduction phases \cite  {grozinger2014molecules}. There are several key phases that honey bees go through. The first is the swarm exodus phase where they leave their current nest, followed by the migration phase where they initiate their search for a new site. When a suitable site is found, the bees form a temporary cluster. This inverted mount is known as a \textit  {bivouac}, and is the subject of study in this paper. b) Examples of bivouacs in nature \cite  {peleg2018collective}, though the structure we eventually aim to emulate is more regular, almost hemispherical in shape. }}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
