\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces  Each snapshot is taken in increments of $25$ bees, up to $150$ bees, for $\alpha = \beta = \sfrac {7}{10}$. a) At the start, bees target the exposed queen. b) Once the queen has been covered, bees land randomly onto the board and begin crawling towards the center. Across stages (c), (d), and (e), more bees are introduced into the system and attach to the structure. Yellow bees are those that are supporting at least one bee, while red bees are those without any bees to support, and are therefore prime candidates to climb downwards as described earlier. f) The bivouac has stabilized and there is minimal climbing and structural fluctuations. }}{10}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:timelapse}{{3}{10}{Each snapshot is taken in increments of $25$ bees, up to $150$ bees, for $\alpha = \beta = \sfrac {7}{10}$. a) At the start, bees target the exposed queen. b) Once the queen has been covered, bees land randomly onto the board and begin crawling towards the center. Across stages (c), (d), and (e), more bees are introduced into the system and attach to the structure. Yellow bees are those that are supporting at least one bee, while red bees are those without any bees to support, and are therefore prime candidates to climb downwards as described earlier. f) The bivouac has stabilized and there is minimal climbing and structural fluctuations}{figure.3}{}}
